<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perdido River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Perdido River is a river on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-the-united-states">Gulf Coast of the United States</a> that forms most of the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. Under French and Spanish rule it marked the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> and West Florida, and in the early 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> claimed the coast as far as the Perdido as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perdido-river.jpg" alt="Perdido River" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adrien Lamarre, U.S. Army Corps of Engineers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USACE_Perdido_Bay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Perdido, known in the past as the Rio Perdido and by its native name of Cassaba, is 65.4 miles (105.3 km) long; perdido is Spanish for lost. It rises in Escambia County in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, about 8 miles (13 km) north-west of Atmore, and flows south about 5 miles (8 km) to latitude 31° N, below which it forms the border between Alabama and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. It then winds generally east-south-east and enters the north end of Perdido Bay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, about 10 miles (16 km) west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a>. It is a designated Outstanding Florida Waters river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1682 to 1763 the Perdido divided the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> from the Spanish colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> received the French lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> and the Perdido as well as Spanish Florida, and split its gains into West Florida and East Florida at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apalachicola-river">Apalachicola River</a>. Twenty years later the Treaty of Paris returned all of Florida to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which kept the British division. In 1800, by the Treaty of San Ildefonso, Spain returned Louisiana to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, keeping the lands east of the Mississippi except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, and in 1803 France sold Louisiana to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>Against the claim of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> held that only the lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> had been returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, so the Gulf coast south of the 31st parallel between the Mississippi and the Perdido remained in dispute. In 1810 the Republic of West Florida declared its independence, and ninety days later United States forces entered its capital, St. Francisville, though this did not reach as far as the Perdido. During the War of 1812 United States forces took the surrender of the Spanish officials at Mobile, and the land west of the river was joined to the Mississippi Territory, from which the Alabama Territory was carved in 1817.</p><p>The dispute ended with the Adams-Onís Treaty of 1819, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; the Spanish government ratified it a couple of years later. In 1822 the Florida Territory was established, with the Perdido as its coastal boundary with the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Perdido River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The strip of Gulf Coast between the Mississippi and <b>Perdido</b> rivers remained Spanish after the Louisiana Purchase, though the United States claimed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-west-florida">Republic of West Florida</a></li></ul></section>
